WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:57.740
- Hello, and welcome to
Ed talks with con academy.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:00:59.860
I'm Kristen DiCerbo, the
Chief Learning Officer

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:00.693
at Khan Academy.

00:01:00.693 --> 00:01:02.910
And I am excited today
to talk to Mike Flanagan,

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:06.210
the CEO of the Mastery
Transcript Consortium.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:08.360
We'll find out what that
is and what it means

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:12.240
for mastery learning, to have
such a such an organization.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:13.433
Before we get started,

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:18.490
Khan Academy is a nonprofit organization,

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:21.180
and we do rely on donors like you,

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.560
to help us keep doing the work we do.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:27.340
If you go to khanacademy.org/donate,

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:30.720
you will find a place where
you can make a contribution

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:33.283
to help us keep doing the
work that we're doing.

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:37.590
Second, we do wanna thank
some of our supporters,

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:41.480
who have helped us with the
COVID-19 crisis response,

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:44.923
including AT&amp;T, General
Motors, and Fastly.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:49.840
Next, if you want to listen
to other conversations

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:53.020
that I've had, or that Sal has
had with interesting guests,

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:55.550
you can find us where
you get your podcasts,

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:58.260
at Homeroom with Sal Podcast.

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:00.930
So you can tune in and listen.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:03.920
And with that, I am excited to welcome,

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:06.350
Mike Flanagan, welcome.

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:08.050
- Greetings, delighted to be here.

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:12.640
- Excellent. So I will
start from the beginning.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:15.780
You're the CEO and Mastery
Transcript Consortium.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:16.960
I bet, when you were a kid,

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:18.910
that wasn't quite what
you said you wanted to be

00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:20.166
when you grew up.

00:02:20.166 --> 00:02:24.313
What is your career trajectory
been like to bring you here?

00:02:25.251 --> 00:02:27.600
- Yeah, that's true.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.350
It would be a really interesting,

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:30.527
toddler walking around being like,

00:02:30.527 --> 00:02:34.380
"I wanna lead a nonprofit
association of schools."

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:38.840
Yeah, so I started my career
as an English teacher.

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:42.360
I taught at an independent
school in Honolulu, Hawaii,

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:43.790
and I taught there for three years.

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:46.690
And it was about as cool as it sounds.

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:48.170
It was really great.

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:50.500
And I moved from teaching into starting

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:51.700
on the path of academia.

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:55.140
And then I did a pivot, and
I wound up in technology.

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:58.020
And I actually wound up doing
a bunch of startup companies,

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:00.020
both with some college classmates,

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:02.970
college classmate of mine, and
other friends along the way.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:04.750
And then things came full circle

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:07.240
where I've now wound up in this role.

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:10.140
That is a really interesting
hybrid of teaching

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:12.750
and pedagogy, and schools, and technology.

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:16.635
And yeah, it wasn't a linear path,

00:03:16.635 --> 00:03:18.390
but it it's one that landed me here.

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:20.290
And I'm pretty excited about it.

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:23.480
And I think that idea of non-linear paths

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:25.230
is actually probably a pretty good fit

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:26.810
for a lot of the things we think about,

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:29.710
in terms of our schools and
the way we try and serve kids.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:33.050
- That totally makes
sense. And I always say,

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:35.860
my career path, I can tell
the story looking backwards,

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:36.693
it makes sense.

00:03:36.693 --> 00:03:38.070
- Yeah.
- But at any given point,

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:40.040
it seems like I was making some left turns

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:43.800
or some weird shifts, yeah. (laughs)

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:46.370
So it's also teacher
appreciation week this week.

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:49.840
So before we get started
talking about your work,

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:51.880
are there any teachers that
you'd like to recognize

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:53.450
from your past?

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:56.025
- Yeah, I grew up and
I was in high school.

00:03:56.025 --> 00:03:58.350
I loved school, right?

00:03:58.350 --> 00:03:59.980
It was my happy place.

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:01.680
But I was very much a STEM kid.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.640
And so my high school science teacher,

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:07.310
Mr. Ezekiel, who taught me AP Chemistry

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:11.730
and gave me, probably, started
me on the road to teaching.

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:14.040
When it was time for us
to get ready for the AP,

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:16.420
he broke the textbook in different chunks,

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:18.370
and had each of us, kind
of give us responsibility

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:19.310
to teach one.

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:20.920
And he gave me Organic Chemistry,

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:22.630
and the light bulb went on.

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:23.463
- Woo!

00:04:23.463 --> 00:04:25.020
- I realized like, oh, I loved,

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:28.540
I was so much more comfortable, confident,

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:30.260
that I really had owned the material,

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:32.300
'cause I had to explain
it to somebody else.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:34.630
That sense of how do
you step outta yourself,

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:36.690
and really figure out how
do I make this make sense

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:38.310
to other people.

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:39.670
The light bulb really went on for me,

00:04:39.670 --> 00:04:40.503
in a lot of different ways.

00:04:40.503 --> 00:04:42.763
So Mr. Ezekiel, if you're
out there, thank you.

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:45.980
- Awesome. Excellent.

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:48.770
So let's talk Mastery
Transcript Consortium,

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:49.610
what is that?

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:51.780
Tell us what it is and what you do.

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:53.210
- Yeah, so we're a nonprofit.

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:54.460
And what we really are is a collection

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.030
of innovative schools.

00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:59.230
We've got a network of just
about 400 high schools,

00:04:59.230 --> 00:05:01.630
mostly here in the US, some international.

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:03.120
And what our schools have in common

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:05.430
is the concept of mastery learning.

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:07.110
And we will unpack mastery
learning, I'm sure,

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:08.150
throughout this discussion.

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:09.610
What it is, and what means?

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:11.183
But really at its core,

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:15.530
it's an embrace of real
learner centered pedagogy,

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:17.370
and a rethinking of assessment.

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:19.020
That's really centered on the idea,

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:21.380
which shouldn't be
radical, but kind of is,

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:24.280
that all children are
capable of learning, right?

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:27.300
And it's our job as school
leaders and system leaders

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:29.180
to create structures and supports,

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:31.340
to make sure that they're
all doing the work,

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:33.370
and really mastering this material

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:34.463
at the highest level.

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:37.860
What happens, and this is
where things get interesting,

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:39.200
this is where the transcript comes in,

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:40.630
and also the consortium,

00:05:40.630 --> 00:05:44.730
is that if you lead a
really innovative school,

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:46.570
and you are adopting a
whole bunch of the models

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:49.070
that we'll talk about,
throughout this discussion,

00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:53.400
you're gonna basically stop
producing a lot of the metrics,

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:55.930
that we think of, as high school.

00:05:55.930 --> 00:05:59.630
So if you're giving credit
based on mastery, proficiency,

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:01.240
it's a really important
tenant of mastery learning.

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:03.630
The basic idea is, hey, don't give credit

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:05.647
based on the number of hours
students have been exposed

00:06:05.647 --> 00:06:07.520
to a subject, give them credit

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:08.930
when they've actually nailed it.

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:10.350
Some students will do it more quickly,

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:12.150
some will need more time.

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:13.860
But once you do that,
you're not giving credit

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:15.420
based on the Carnegie Unit anymore.

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:18.270
You're not giving credit
based on credit hours.

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:21.040
When you think about the
way we assess students,

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:23.106
it's just normal, traditional grading.

00:06:23.106 --> 00:06:26.040
Assumes that kids will be
distributed on a curve.

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:29.260
Some kids are good at
school, some kids are not.

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:31.040
Mastery learning rejects that principle.

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:33.240
We basically say, "No, all
kids are capable of learning."

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:35.460
Some will learn faster than
others, and that's fine,

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:37.880
but they can all be really proficient,

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:39.800
and some will even be advanced.

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:42.855
And in that kind of model, what is a C-?

00:06:42.855 --> 00:06:45.790
A C- doesn't even exist
anymore, in those models.

00:06:45.790 --> 00:06:48.280
So you take those two
things, credit hours,

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:52.280
traditional grades, you put
those in the parking lot,

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:54.390
And then you also take this
idea of like, what is school?

00:06:54.390 --> 00:06:55.840
What should kids be learning?

00:06:56.950 --> 00:06:58.150
And traditional transcripts

00:06:58.150 --> 00:07:00.300
really think about
classic academic subjects,

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:01.910
English, Math, Science, Social Studies.

00:07:01.910 --> 00:07:03.679
There's nothing wrong with
those things, they're great.

00:07:03.679 --> 00:07:06.120
But there's a lot more
that we want kids to learn

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:07.110
and be excellent at.

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:08.420
Particularly, if we're gonna prepare them

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:10.190
for a really interesting,

00:07:10.190 --> 00:07:12.290
and maybe volatile and uncertain future.

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:14.040
We wanna give 'em 21st century skills.

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:15.910
We want 'em have opportunities

00:07:15.910 --> 00:07:18.510
to do deep interdisciplinary learning.

00:07:18.510 --> 00:07:19.550
There's literally no place

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:22.880
to capture those kinds of
21st century competencies

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:24.240
in a traditional transcript.

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:26.610
So what you have is this
really odd phenomenon,

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:28.630
where schools that are in some ways,

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:30.300
being the best possible school.

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:33.120
That brain science says you can design,

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:36.920
is now producing kids with student records

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:39.000
that don't make any sense to college.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:43.310
Because college is used to
thinking about credits, grades,

00:07:43.310 --> 00:07:46.020
and the classic academic disciplines.

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:46.990
That's a big issue.

00:07:46.990 --> 00:07:50.280
I'm, as a parent, as an educator,

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:52.340
I think these schools are awesome.

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:54.560
I want as many of them as possible to grow

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:56.750
and propagate around the country.

00:07:56.750 --> 00:07:59.210
I want every kid to be
able to have access to one.

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:00.043
If the message is,

00:08:00.043 --> 00:08:02.160
"Oh, you can go to this
really innovative school,

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:03.460
but you might not get into college

00:08:03.460 --> 00:08:04.840
'cause you look different,"

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:06.650
that's not gonna be good for adoption.

00:08:06.650 --> 00:08:09.450
So college is not the only,

00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:11.920
or even the most important destination,

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:13.666
but it's definitely a blocking issue.

00:08:13.666 --> 00:08:17.690
if we cannot show that
these innovative schools

00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:21.330
can still give kids access
to higher ed campuses,

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:24.370
that's gonna be bad for us
as system change agents.

00:08:24.370 --> 00:08:26.590
And so that's what the
transcript is designed to do.

00:08:26.590 --> 00:08:29.410
It's a translation tool
that takes the models

00:08:29.410 --> 00:08:33.720
that our schools have, and
through our best effort,

00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:37.000
presents them to higher ed
in a way that's interactive,

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:38.550
usable, and hopefully
makes a lot of sense,

00:08:38.550 --> 00:08:42.303
and helps 'em understand where
these K12 schools are headed.

00:08:43.830 --> 00:08:44.663
- Got it.

00:08:44.663 --> 00:08:45.496
That makes sense.

00:08:45.496 --> 00:08:48.820
So to unpack some of that,
let's start with the pedagogy

00:08:48.820 --> 00:08:50.160
and the mastery learning piece.

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:52.646
So part of the reason
I wanted to talk to you

00:08:52.646 --> 00:08:56.682
is because Khan Academy also
really believes in that idea

00:08:56.682 --> 00:09:00.840
of it's it's okay to
fail your first attempt.

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:03.800
It's not about what score
you get on one attempt,

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:07.760
it's about reaching a high
level of proficiency on a skill

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:09.630
and how you get there.

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:12.420
What are some of the things
that you see as foundational

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:16.040
for mastery learning and
some of the key tenants?

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:16.873
- Yeah.

00:09:16.873 --> 00:09:17.910
Well, I mean there's foundational,

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:20.754
and then there's also, what
becomes possible, right?

00:09:20.754 --> 00:09:21.587
- Yeah.

00:09:21.587 --> 00:09:24.010
- So foundational is
definitely this idea of

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:27.730
students progress when they've
mastered a particular skill,

00:09:27.730 --> 00:09:28.970
and not before.

00:09:28.970 --> 00:09:30.100
And if you're going to do that,

00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:33.610
that necessitates a more
personalized approach, right?

00:09:33.610 --> 00:09:36.230
You can't have kids
learning at different rates,

00:09:36.230 --> 00:09:38.750
and then expect them
all to move in lockstep.

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:42.420
They're just fundamentally,
it doesn't doesn't compute.

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:44.290
So that has implications
for your school day,

00:09:44.290 --> 00:09:46.020
your school schedule.

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:47.920
It implies certain kinds of technologies

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:49.940
to reduce the administrative burden

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:52.560
on school leaders and faculty.

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:53.640
But at the end of the day,

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:55.890
personalized approach is really key.

00:09:55.890 --> 00:09:57.900
Thinking about those cross-disciplinary

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:00.400
21st century competencies and skills,

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:03.500
finding ways of re-entering
those into what kids do,

00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:06.560
and helping them understand
that a lot of things

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:09.300
that used to be tacit in school, right?

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:11.300
What does it mean to be a good leader?

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:12.133
What does it mean

00:10:12.133 --> 00:10:14.770
to really collaborate
effectively on a team?

00:10:14.770 --> 00:10:16.590
What does it mean to be creative,

00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:18.970
or to solve problems creatively?

00:10:18.970 --> 00:10:21.230
Those aren't like intangible things.

00:10:21.230 --> 00:10:22.170
They can be taught.

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:26.090
You can describe to a pretty
high level of precision.

00:10:26.090 --> 00:10:28.490
What good performance
looks like in those things?

00:10:28.490 --> 00:10:31.720
And if you start a process of
exposing kids to those things,

00:10:31.720 --> 00:10:33.500
as early as eighth, ninth grade,

00:10:33.500 --> 00:10:35.140
there's no reason why they all can't get

00:10:35.140 --> 00:10:36.580
to really high levels of performance

00:10:36.580 --> 00:10:38.100
by the time they leave high school.

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:41.080
As opposed to sort of focusing
on your academic subjects

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:43.733
and hoping they pick up
that stuff along the way.

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:45.940
So those two things,

00:10:45.940 --> 00:10:48.180
21st century skills that
are cross-disciplinary,

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:51.090
credit based on
proficiency, not seat time,

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:53.080
those are the essentials.

00:10:53.080 --> 00:10:56.240
And then what becomes possible
is a lot of opportunities

00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:58.180
to have kids in engage in deeper,

00:10:58.180 --> 00:11:00.060
more purpose-driven learning.

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:01.570
So if you have students,

00:11:01.570 --> 00:11:03.030
if they're given more choice, right?

00:11:03.030 --> 00:11:04.850
'Cause they're on a more
personalized approach,

00:11:04.850 --> 00:11:07.060
and they're not wedded to
studying the same things

00:11:07.060 --> 00:11:09.420
as all their classmates at the same times,

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:10.253
then there's no reason

00:11:10.253 --> 00:11:12.100
they can't do more project-based learning.

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:15.240
Do long form capstones
or independent studies.

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:17.670
You'll hear me use the word,
we, a lot in these discussions

00:11:17.670 --> 00:11:20.030
because we've got this amazing
network of schools, right?

00:11:20.030 --> 00:11:23.160
And they're private schools,
public schools, districts,

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:25.350
there's rural, urban, big, small,

00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:29.600
it's amazing heterogeneous
group, and that's a good thing.

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:31.950
But a lot of them have kids doing project

00:11:31.950 --> 00:11:33.160
and problem-based learning.

00:11:33.160 --> 00:11:35.442
Either individually or in teams,

00:11:35.442 --> 00:11:38.720
they're having kids solve
real world problems.

00:11:38.720 --> 00:11:42.360
Not like cookbook science,
like you would do in lab,

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:43.940
where it's like, oh, we're gonna measure,

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:45.920
drop something and see
how long it takes to fall.

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:48.020
Well, surprise, gravity's constant.

00:11:48.020 --> 00:11:50.210
We know how long it's gonna take to fall.

00:11:50.210 --> 00:11:53.160
We're gonna have 'em solve
messy problems, right?

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:55.360
There's a reason a lot of
our schools embrace things

00:11:55.360 --> 00:11:57.930
like design thinking and entrepreneurship.

00:11:57.930 --> 00:11:59.900
It's not just 'cause they're hip,

00:11:59.900 --> 00:12:01.780
it's because they're cyclical.

00:12:01.780 --> 00:12:04.170
They assume a certain amount of iteration,

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:07.840
like try, test, iterate, repeat, adjust.

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:11.560
Those things, we think are really powerful

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:13.660
because they create that space to fail

00:12:13.660 --> 00:12:15.380
in a constructive environment, right?

00:12:15.380 --> 00:12:17.040
To that, it's okay not to be right.

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:19.580
Your first attempt is not
always gonna be your best one.

00:12:19.580 --> 00:12:21.510
So those are, I think, are
that combination of things

00:12:21.510 --> 00:12:22.910
that are essential to mastery learning

00:12:22.910 --> 00:12:26.470
and things that become
possible and pretty exciting.

00:12:26.470 --> 00:12:29.362
And frankly, more
interesting for students.

00:12:29.362 --> 00:12:33.460
We talk a lot about the mission

00:12:33.460 --> 00:12:35.900
of Mastery Transcript Schools.

00:12:35.900 --> 00:12:37.360
Well, really all schools I think,

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:40.473
is to prepare young adults
for what they want to do next.

00:12:41.460 --> 00:12:43.360
Whether it's college, whether it's career,

00:12:43.360 --> 00:12:45.350
whether it's kind of something in between.

00:12:45.350 --> 00:12:47.500
What I think is really
interesting is that for many kids

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:49.020
in traditional schools,

00:12:49.020 --> 00:12:52.060
the hardest word in that sentence is want.

00:12:52.060 --> 00:12:53.890
what do you want to do next?

00:12:53.890 --> 00:12:56.550
We don't ask kids what
they want to do very often,

00:12:56.550 --> 00:12:59.130
in traditional schools,
we tell them what to do.

00:12:59.130 --> 00:13:01.440
And as a result, they
internalize this message

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:03.300
that to be a student, to be a learner,

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:06.400
is to show up and do what you're told.

00:13:06.400 --> 00:13:08.160
And then we launch them into the world,

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:09.630
hopefully, we want them to be successful,

00:13:09.630 --> 00:13:11.430
and it turns out that's not
the way the world works.

00:13:11.430 --> 00:13:13.710
You have to figure out what
you're gonna do, specifically.

00:13:13.710 --> 00:13:15.622
So yeah.
- Yeah.

00:13:15.622 --> 00:13:17.800
- That's like the opening...

00:13:17.800 --> 00:13:20.460
- It feels like one of
those 21st century skills

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:22.490
is actually learning how to learn,

00:13:22.490 --> 00:13:25.658
and being an owner of
your own learning and...

00:13:25.658 --> 00:13:26.491
- Yeah.

00:13:26.491 --> 00:13:27.980
- Knowing how to set goals for yourself,

00:13:27.980 --> 00:13:30.390
and see, monitor if you're on track.

00:13:30.390 --> 00:13:31.223
- Yeah.

00:13:31.223 --> 00:13:34.100
- And doing all of those things,
to understand how to learn.

00:13:34.100 --> 00:13:39.000
- Yeah. I think we started,
like phase one of MTC

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:40.870
was very much focused on showing

00:13:40.870 --> 00:13:43.840
that kids could get into college

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:46.911
without these traditional metrics, right?

00:13:46.911 --> 00:13:50.390
And one of the reasons we've
been really successful,

00:13:50.390 --> 00:13:52.123
even with a small number of schools

00:13:52.123 --> 00:13:53.990
that have fully adopted our transcript.

00:13:53.990 --> 00:13:56.450
We've gotten kids into 250
colleges and universities,

00:13:56.450 --> 00:13:58.930
some of the most selective
and also open enrollment,

00:13:58.930 --> 00:14:02.230
two year technical programs,
everything in between.

00:14:02.230 --> 00:14:04.150
But I'm always really
quick to tell people,

00:14:04.150 --> 00:14:06.270
it's not actually because
of the transcript.

00:14:06.270 --> 00:14:09.850
The transcript does not
give you an advantage

00:14:09.850 --> 00:14:13.490
in getting into MIT or to Caltech.

00:14:13.490 --> 00:14:16.050
But going to a mastery
learning-based school,

00:14:16.050 --> 00:14:19.590
like one in which you're
encouraged throughout four years

00:14:19.590 --> 00:14:23.130
to really do deep work, to think about it,

00:14:23.130 --> 00:14:25.730
reflect on that work with
an adult in your life.

00:14:25.730 --> 00:14:26.830
Maybe write something about it,

00:14:26.830 --> 00:14:28.290
maybe present it to a group,

00:14:28.290 --> 00:14:30.240
and then make a decision about

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:32.040
what you wanna do next with that.

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:32.930
Maybe it's iterate.

00:14:32.930 --> 00:14:33.810
Maybe it's revise it.

00:14:33.810 --> 00:14:36.160
Maybe it's go a different
direction, try something new.

00:14:36.160 --> 00:14:38.620
If you do that cycle really purposefully,

00:14:38.620 --> 00:14:41.300
and with a lot of feedback in coaching,

00:14:41.300 --> 00:14:43.410
by the time you get out the other end,

00:14:43.410 --> 00:14:46.060
your metacognitive skills,
what you just said, Kris,

00:14:46.060 --> 00:14:49.410
learning how to learn, they
are absolutely super powered.

00:14:49.410 --> 00:14:51.661
And so when you talk
to our kids, our kids,

00:14:51.661 --> 00:14:54.170
the kids that go to these schools,

00:14:54.170 --> 00:14:56.060
they just make amazing applicants,

00:14:56.060 --> 00:14:58.010
whether they're applying
for an internship or a job,

00:14:58.010 --> 00:14:59.980
or applying to college.

00:14:59.980 --> 00:15:03.520
Because they've got
real, authentic answers

00:15:03.520 --> 00:15:05.020
to some of these essential questions.

00:15:05.020 --> 00:15:05.860
What do you want to do?

00:15:05.860 --> 00:15:09.450
If we give you a slot
at this campus, right?

00:15:09.450 --> 00:15:10.590
Where you could do any sort of things,

00:15:10.590 --> 00:15:12.240
major in all kinds of different subjects,

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:14.750
and study with some of
the world's best experts,

00:15:14.750 --> 00:15:15.920
what are you gonna do here?

00:15:15.920 --> 00:15:17.610
What will you do to take advantage of it?

00:15:17.610 --> 00:15:19.810
If we give you an
internship at our company,

00:15:21.150 --> 00:15:22.150
how can you contribute?

00:15:22.150 --> 00:15:23.790
What are some things that are interesting?

00:15:23.790 --> 00:15:26.960
Why do you wanna work here
versus somewhere else?

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:28.420
They have those answers

00:15:28.420 --> 00:15:30.120
because it's not the first
time they've been asked

00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:32.260
those kinds of really deep questions

00:15:32.260 --> 00:15:33.470
about what they're doing and why.

00:15:33.470 --> 00:15:36.830
And that for us, is
that metacognitive move,

00:15:36.830 --> 00:15:38.653
learning how to learn that you described.

00:15:38.653 --> 00:15:40.910
- Yeah. Yeah, absolutely.

00:15:40.910 --> 00:15:43.825
So let's paint a picture then,

00:15:43.825 --> 00:15:47.640
if you don't have take
a test, get a grade,

00:15:47.640 --> 00:15:51.200
what does it look like
to be able to demonstrate

00:15:51.200 --> 00:15:53.010
that you have have these skills?

00:15:53.010 --> 00:15:54.910
- Yeah, I mean, I think,

00:15:54.910 --> 00:15:56.650
our schools still have tests, right?

00:15:56.650 --> 00:15:59.190
Like I don't want to
paint a utopian picture

00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:00.700
where our kids like never look at anything

00:16:00.700 --> 00:16:02.400
that looks like an exam.

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:04.205
You still need like academic content,

00:16:04.205 --> 00:16:05.920
and there's a place for that.

00:16:05.920 --> 00:16:07.670
But I do think that what you'll see,

00:16:07.670 --> 00:16:10.296
and much more often is
much less focused on

00:16:10.296 --> 00:16:13.230
high stakes as assessment,

00:16:13.230 --> 00:16:15.810
and much more judicious use of that,

00:16:15.810 --> 00:16:19.140
and much more focus on
feedback, right, on.

00:16:19.140 --> 00:16:20.840
So what we wanna do is have students

00:16:20.840 --> 00:16:24.060
really engaged in real work,

00:16:24.060 --> 00:16:27.530
and then have them giving
feedback to one another,

00:16:27.530 --> 00:16:30.630
teaching them how to give
feedback in a constructive way.

00:16:30.630 --> 00:16:31.680
That's a pretty important skill.

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:33.180
It's a life skill.

00:16:33.180 --> 00:16:34.660
I think we've all, unfortunately,

00:16:34.660 --> 00:16:37.060
had experiences where
we've worked with people,

00:16:37.060 --> 00:16:38.430
who maybe didn't have that skill,

00:16:38.430 --> 00:16:40.070
didn't know how to give
constructive feedback.

00:16:40.070 --> 00:16:43.780
And that's not like a recipe
for like awesome performance.

00:16:43.780 --> 00:16:47.380
So helping kids get real
feedback, both from one another,

00:16:47.380 --> 00:16:48.343
and from adults.

00:16:49.270 --> 00:16:52.230
And then creating really clear structures

00:16:52.230 --> 00:16:53.710
for what they're being assessed against.

00:16:53.710 --> 00:16:54.850
So I think one of the things

00:16:54.850 --> 00:16:57.360
that every single one of our schools.

00:16:57.360 --> 00:17:02.360
In fact, I would say, you can't
use the mastery transcript,

00:17:02.420 --> 00:17:04.580
or really any competency based transcript,

00:17:04.580 --> 00:17:08.440
unless you as a school, as
a learning organization,

00:17:08.440 --> 00:17:11.240
have a really, really good
portrait of a graduate, right?

00:17:11.240 --> 00:17:14.210
And so portrait of a graduate
for us is the term of art

00:17:14.210 --> 00:17:17.760
for a really well designed
set of competencies.

00:17:17.760 --> 00:17:20.400
And ideally, what they've been created

00:17:20.400 --> 00:17:22.250
in a backwards design process, right?

00:17:22.250 --> 00:17:24.240
You ask yourself the essential question of

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:27.500
what do we want our kids to be able to do

00:17:27.500 --> 00:17:29.130
by the time they leave our campus, right?

00:17:29.130 --> 00:17:30.720
What do we want 'em to be able to do

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:34.170
by the time they cross the
stage and grab that diploma.

00:17:34.170 --> 00:17:37.223
And so for us, that portrait of a graduate

00:17:37.223 --> 00:17:40.130
is really the cornerstone to that,

00:17:40.130 --> 00:17:43.740
because every single one of
those competencies, in turn,

00:17:43.740 --> 00:17:45.920
should have really clear descriptions.

00:17:45.920 --> 00:17:48.330
Like with a lot of
Blooms Taxonomy language,

00:17:48.330 --> 00:17:52.383
about what those demonstrated
behaviors look like.

00:17:53.630 --> 00:17:55.840
One of the real challenges
of traditional grading

00:17:55.840 --> 00:17:58.120
is that sometimes students
don't actually really know

00:17:58.120 --> 00:18:00.120
what they're being graded on.

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:01.470
And so I think one of the advantages

00:18:01.470 --> 00:18:02.807
of so-called standard
space grading, right?

00:18:02.807 --> 00:18:04.920
And if you look at the work
that Jill Feldman has done

00:18:04.920 --> 00:18:07.490
in Grading for Equity,
it's this idea that,

00:18:07.490 --> 00:18:09.590
oh, these things have
to be super explicit.

00:18:09.590 --> 00:18:13.330
Everyone should know on day
one, what good looks like?

00:18:13.330 --> 00:18:15.530
And when you see it spelled
out that way you realize

00:18:15.530 --> 00:18:17.270
how often that isn't true, sometimes,

00:18:17.270 --> 00:18:19.100
in traditional grading environments.

00:18:19.100 --> 00:18:22.190
The other thing is that you
should really be assessing

00:18:22.190 --> 00:18:25.330
what it is essential to
the school environment,

00:18:25.330 --> 00:18:27.110
like the learning, as opposed to things

00:18:27.110 --> 00:18:29.450
like behavior and compliance.

00:18:29.450 --> 00:18:31.640
There's a lot of like
traditional grading models

00:18:31.640 --> 00:18:33.900
that blur the line between
classroom management,

00:18:33.900 --> 00:18:36.500
and actually measuring
what kids can actually do,

00:18:36.500 --> 00:18:38.060
and what they're actually learning.

00:18:38.060 --> 00:18:39.160
And it's really important to stay,

00:18:39.160 --> 00:18:42.012
we think, really squarely
on one side or the other.

00:18:42.012 --> 00:18:43.262
And then lastly, I think,

00:18:45.423 --> 00:18:46.320
so standards based grading is great,

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:50.270
but it is the beginning of
a school evolution process.

00:18:50.270 --> 00:18:52.283
True mastery learning is,

00:18:53.313 --> 00:18:54.540
if you think about it as a Venn Diagram,

00:18:54.540 --> 00:18:56.140
standards-based grading
is a smaller circle,

00:18:56.140 --> 00:18:59.190
and mastery-based assessment
is in a larger circle,

00:18:59.190 --> 00:19:00.023
around it.

00:19:01.140 --> 00:19:01.973
- Got it.

00:19:01.973 --> 00:19:03.070
That makes sense.

00:19:03.070 --> 00:19:05.984
So lots of questions,
but we do have a question

00:19:05.984 --> 00:19:10.520
coming in asking about,
for the average teacher,

00:19:10.520 --> 00:19:13.300
who's maybe in a traditional school,

00:19:13.300 --> 00:19:16.333
but wants to begin doing some of this.

00:19:17.188 --> 00:19:19.940
How can they bring some of these ideas

00:19:19.940 --> 00:19:22.410
into their current practice,

00:19:22.410 --> 00:19:24.820
when maybe the system isn't quite set up

00:19:24.820 --> 00:19:26.040
for the doing it, yet?

00:19:26.040 --> 00:19:26.873
- Yeah.

00:19:26.873 --> 00:19:29.630
And I think I really appreciate
whoever asked that question

00:19:29.630 --> 00:19:31.920
because it it's really
important to take a step back.

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:34.130
And if you say, well, why do we even have,

00:19:34.130 --> 00:19:36.745
or why do we even need a
mastery transcript consortium?

00:19:36.745 --> 00:19:39.910
Why not just have
teachers start to do this?

00:19:39.910 --> 00:19:44.660
It's that the system change
is really hard, right?

00:19:44.660 --> 00:19:48.220
We have to change both our
policies in our 50 states.

00:19:48.220 --> 00:19:50.270
We have to change the technologies

00:19:50.270 --> 00:19:52.030
that our teachers, and administrators,

00:19:52.030 --> 00:19:53.590
and learners have access to.

00:19:53.590 --> 00:19:56.040
And so one of the reasons
we're a nonprofit,

00:19:56.040 --> 00:19:58.360
and trying to drive
system change over time

00:19:58.360 --> 00:20:00.450
is because we know that
this is a long road.

00:20:00.450 --> 00:20:01.610
There's a lot of work to do.

00:20:01.610 --> 00:20:04.020
And we're really glad
to have so many allies

00:20:04.020 --> 00:20:04.853
in the space, right?

00:20:04.853 --> 00:20:07.414
We're part of a much larger
and really vibrant ecosystem

00:20:07.414 --> 00:20:09.480
of nonprofit players

00:20:09.480 --> 00:20:11.537
that are all working on
competency-based education

00:20:11.537 --> 00:20:15.090
and personalized learning and,
and mastery-based assessment.

00:20:15.090 --> 00:20:18.200
And all pulling schools, we
think in a good direction.

00:20:18.200 --> 00:20:19.840
That said, if you are a teacher,

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:21.681
and you are out on an island, right?

00:20:21.681 --> 00:20:23.750
You're the only person at
your school, theoretically,

00:20:23.750 --> 00:20:26.410
who is interested in rethinking
grading and assessment.

00:20:26.410 --> 00:20:28.200
There are things you can do.

00:20:28.200 --> 00:20:30.750
Contract-based grading is a model

00:20:30.750 --> 00:20:33.290
that is most mastery-like, I think.

00:20:33.290 --> 00:20:36.050
Given if you have in the confines

00:20:36.050 --> 00:20:39.000
of a traditional classroom
and a traditional schedule.

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:41.110
So you don't have autonomy, necessarily,

00:20:41.110 --> 00:20:43.540
over how long your class is gonna run,

00:20:43.540 --> 00:20:45.690
or maybe even, what
your kids are studying.

00:20:45.690 --> 00:20:48.290
What you can do is, on day one,

00:20:48.290 --> 00:20:49.970
be really clear with students

00:20:49.970 --> 00:20:52.440
about what the standards
are for them to achieve,

00:20:52.440 --> 00:20:54.940
say a B or an A.

00:20:54.940 --> 00:20:56.540
And then work with them
throughout the year,

00:20:56.540 --> 00:20:57.820
to help them understand where they are,

00:20:57.820 --> 00:21:01.060
in terms of getting it,
and then giving them space.

00:21:01.060 --> 00:21:03.670
And this is a real key principle
of standards-based grading

00:21:03.670 --> 00:21:04.750
and grading for equity.

00:21:04.750 --> 00:21:06.930
Giving them space to retake and retry,

00:21:06.930 --> 00:21:08.370
kind of high stakes assessments,

00:21:08.370 --> 00:21:10.900
whether they're big projects, or tests.

00:21:10.900 --> 00:21:14.087
And there's a really amazing
array of literature on that.

00:21:14.087 --> 00:21:17.090
And MTC does not own that space, right?

00:21:17.090 --> 00:21:20.570
If you go to just even on social media,

00:21:20.570 --> 00:21:23.440
there are incredible networks
of teachers self organizing,

00:21:23.440 --> 00:21:27.060
around teachers going gradeless,
the Ungrading Movement.

00:21:27.060 --> 00:21:29.640
There is a real ground swell of educators

00:21:29.640 --> 00:21:32.530
who realize, oh, we've gotten
ourselves into a situation

00:21:32.530 --> 00:21:35.880
where the thing that is
supposed to measure learning,

00:21:35.880 --> 00:21:38.300
which is assessment grading,

00:21:38.300 --> 00:21:39.940
has now kind of in a
weird way loop to back,

00:21:39.940 --> 00:21:43.700
where it's actually pulling
and pushing against learning.

00:21:43.700 --> 00:21:45.220
And I don't think anybody ever designed

00:21:45.220 --> 00:21:46.650
and wanted that to be the case.

00:21:46.650 --> 00:21:48.310
I think we should all
agree that our assessments

00:21:48.310 --> 00:21:51.500
should help kids learn,
not push against them.

00:21:51.500 --> 00:21:53.170
And I think there's growing
recognition around that,

00:21:53.170 --> 00:21:55.530
and a lot of resources available.

00:21:55.530 --> 00:21:56.500
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:21:56.500 --> 00:21:57.333
Excellent.

00:21:57.333 --> 00:21:58.166
Thank you.

00:21:58.166 --> 00:22:02.690
So I know, I'm also on the
board of the Khan Lab School,

00:22:02.690 --> 00:22:06.090
whose been one of the
original users of this,

00:22:06.090 --> 00:22:10.277
and when I first saw the
transcript itself, I was like,

00:22:10.277 --> 00:22:11.520
"Oh, this is amazing."

00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:13.090
Can you share with us a little bit about

00:22:13.090 --> 00:22:15.600
just what a transcript looks like in MTC?

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:18.110
- Yeah, and so I will just say,

00:22:18.110 --> 00:22:20.980
we are very lucky to
have a very simple URL,

00:22:20.980 --> 00:22:22.499
it's mastery.org.

00:22:22.499 --> 00:22:24.160
So if you want to kind of learn more about

00:22:24.160 --> 00:22:25.330
what that transcript looks like,

00:22:25.330 --> 00:22:26.703
and see a version of it.

00:22:28.780 --> 00:22:32.061
At the core, a mastery
transcript is a visualization

00:22:32.061 --> 00:22:34.670
of a school's competency model.

00:22:34.670 --> 00:22:36.610
And we spent a lot of time,

00:22:36.610 --> 00:22:41.440
before we even built, sketched,
the first transcript system,

00:22:41.440 --> 00:22:43.311
we spent a lot of time
talking to higher education.

00:22:43.311 --> 00:22:45.020
Our team, we were really lucky.

00:22:45.020 --> 00:22:48.080
We were able to sign some
confidentiality agreements

00:22:48.080 --> 00:22:50.210
and actually sit in admissions on offices,

00:22:50.210 --> 00:22:53.070
and listen as the readers were looking

00:22:53.070 --> 00:22:54.740
at actual student folders.

00:22:54.740 --> 00:22:55.810
And what you realize is,

00:22:55.810 --> 00:22:59.690
that even though the
colleges vary quite a lot,

00:22:59.690 --> 00:23:02.130
there's a workflow of
what they're looking for.

00:23:02.130 --> 00:23:04.040
The first question they always have is,

00:23:04.040 --> 00:23:05.600
what is this school?

00:23:05.600 --> 00:23:06.850
What is this program like?

00:23:06.850 --> 00:23:10.840
I want to understand the
context of where this applicant

00:23:10.840 --> 00:23:13.140
has done their highschool work.

00:23:13.140 --> 00:23:15.780
And so traditionally, they
might look at a school profile.

00:23:15.780 --> 00:23:17.340
They might then look
back at the transcript,

00:23:17.340 --> 00:23:19.680
and might then look,
try, and cross-reference.

00:23:19.680 --> 00:23:22.200
So for us, the front page
of the mastery transcript

00:23:22.200 --> 00:23:25.510
is a visualization of that
school's competencies.

00:23:25.510 --> 00:23:26.343
And by looking at it,

00:23:26.343 --> 00:23:28.530
you can instantly tell if
we've done our job right.

00:23:28.530 --> 00:23:30.470
What that shape of the school is, right?

00:23:30.470 --> 00:23:33.040
Or you can see this is where
they spend the most time.

00:23:33.040 --> 00:23:35.250
We're really big believers that students,

00:23:35.250 --> 00:23:37.880
learners in mastery-based
schools should develop

00:23:37.880 --> 00:23:39.900
what we call like a jagged profile, right?

00:23:39.900 --> 00:23:42.420
So if you give kids time
and space to go deep

00:23:42.420 --> 00:23:44.220
in things they really care about,

00:23:44.220 --> 00:23:45.690
they'll go in different directions.

00:23:45.690 --> 00:23:46.523
That's a good thing.

00:23:46.523 --> 00:23:48.530
That's a feature, not a bug.

00:23:48.530 --> 00:23:51.530
And so schools can have
jagged profiles, as well.

00:23:51.530 --> 00:23:54.630
And so we want those to
literally pop off the page.

00:23:54.630 --> 00:23:57.730
The second thing you'll look
at then is that jagged profile,

00:23:57.730 --> 00:24:00.950
the competencies for the
student, in particular.

00:24:00.950 --> 00:24:04.650
If every student who got the
diploma earned these over here,

00:24:04.650 --> 00:24:07.190
then individual students will
go in different directions.

00:24:07.190 --> 00:24:09.910
And we have a separate
section that visualizes that.

00:24:09.910 --> 00:24:11.660
We call them sort of advanced competencies

00:24:11.660 --> 00:24:13.553
or advanced mastery credits.

00:24:14.750 --> 00:24:17.364
And so in that space is
really where we can start

00:24:17.364 --> 00:24:22.110
to help colleges do what
they say they want to do,

00:24:22.110 --> 00:24:23.940
which is match their applicants,

00:24:23.940 --> 00:24:26.830
based on fit to the programs
they're applying to,

00:24:26.830 --> 00:24:29.550
and the campuses that they're applying to.

00:24:29.550 --> 00:24:31.550
And so if you have students

00:24:31.550 --> 00:24:35.840
who say that they're applying
to a nursing program, right,

00:24:35.840 --> 00:24:38.510
you're gonna look for
some STEM background,

00:24:38.510 --> 00:24:40.760
but you're also gonna look,
ideally I would think,

00:24:40.760 --> 00:24:42.600
for some kind of service orientation.

00:24:42.600 --> 00:24:45.820
So that student has started
to do some things that show,

00:24:45.820 --> 00:24:47.700
yeah, they really care about other people.

00:24:47.700 --> 00:24:50.700
They wanna spend their time,
maybe it's volunteer work,

00:24:50.700 --> 00:24:52.283
maybe it's caring for siblings.

00:24:53.260 --> 00:24:55.990
But making moves choices in their life

00:24:55.990 --> 00:24:59.250
That show that they've got an orientation

00:24:59.250 --> 00:25:00.560
towards helping other people out,

00:25:00.560 --> 00:25:02.570
that's a pretty big predictor,

00:25:02.570 --> 00:25:04.280
whether he or she is gonna be successful

00:25:04.280 --> 00:25:05.770
in a nursing environment.

00:25:05.770 --> 00:25:07.140
So I think that's really important.

00:25:07.140 --> 00:25:09.317
And you can imagine
other examples like that.

00:25:09.317 --> 00:25:13.690
so what that fit between
where the student has chosen

00:25:13.690 --> 00:25:15.600
to go deep and what they
say they care about,

00:25:15.600 --> 00:25:17.280
and what they want to
go deeply in college.

00:25:17.280 --> 00:25:19.780
When you see that alignment,

00:25:19.780 --> 00:25:23.550
it helps you identify that
in, it clicks really quickly

00:25:23.550 --> 00:25:27.121
in ways that honestly
traditional transcripts don't.

00:25:27.121 --> 00:25:28.940
One of the strange things

00:25:28.940 --> 00:25:30.590
about a traditional schooling model

00:25:30.590 --> 00:25:33.050
is that as students get more successful,

00:25:33.050 --> 00:25:34.480
quote, unquote in school,

00:25:34.480 --> 00:25:37.090
their transcripts actually
start to look really similar.

00:25:37.090 --> 00:25:40.410
They're all taking the same
AP classes or honors classes.

00:25:40.410 --> 00:25:42.880
They're all getting 4.0 grades.

00:25:42.880 --> 00:25:44.910
And so the thing that is most important,

00:25:44.910 --> 00:25:46.120
the thing that's supposed to capture,

00:25:46.120 --> 00:25:48.380
like four years of high school,

00:25:48.380 --> 00:25:50.100
actually doesn't tell you much at all

00:25:50.100 --> 00:25:51.710
about what makes them unique or different.

00:25:51.710 --> 00:25:52.543
And so that's one of the problems

00:25:52.543 --> 00:25:54.463
that we tried to lean into, pretty hard.

00:25:55.720 --> 00:25:56.680
- That totally makes sense.

00:25:56.680 --> 00:26:00.100
And so what's been the
reaction from the colleges

00:26:00.100 --> 00:26:02.238
and universities that have
gotten these transcripts?

00:26:02.238 --> 00:26:03.071
- Yeah.

00:26:03.961 --> 00:26:08.961
So what I always say are,
our reach has been limited,

00:26:10.330 --> 00:26:11.870
but our uptake has been really good.

00:26:11.870 --> 00:26:13.250
And here's what I mean.

00:26:13.250 --> 00:26:16.900
There were a million distinct applications

00:26:16.900 --> 00:26:17.733
that got processed

00:26:17.733 --> 00:26:20.220
through the common application
last season, right?

00:26:20.220 --> 00:26:22.520
So we have a lot of work in front of us.

00:26:22.520 --> 00:26:23.353
If we want 1%...

00:26:23.353 --> 00:26:24.253
- Yeah, it's a big number.

00:26:24.253 --> 00:26:25.724
- (indistinct) transcripts out there.

00:26:25.724 --> 00:26:30.660
And so if you were to go to any,

00:26:30.660 --> 00:26:33.550
pick a random folder reader, if you will,

00:26:33.550 --> 00:26:34.797
an admissions officer at a campus,

00:26:34.797 --> 00:26:36.860
"Hey, have you, have you
seen a mastery transcript?"

00:26:36.860 --> 00:26:38.340
Statistically, almost certainly,

00:26:38.340 --> 00:26:39.173
they're gonna be like,

00:26:39.173 --> 00:26:40.899
"Nope, I have no idea what
you're talking about."

00:26:40.899 --> 00:26:41.732
- Right.

00:26:41.732 --> 00:26:44.040
- But for the schools that
got a mastery transcript,

00:26:44.040 --> 00:26:46.180
we've got a really good
track record of getting

00:26:46.180 --> 00:26:49.530
in front of those proactively,
and talking to schools,

00:26:49.530 --> 00:26:52.630
college readers about that experience.

00:26:52.630 --> 00:26:54.680
And it's pretty obvious, right?

00:26:54.680 --> 00:26:56.057
If I call you in the abstract and say,

00:26:56.057 --> 00:26:57.280
"Would you like to do a focus group

00:26:57.280 --> 00:26:58.667
about this strange
alternative transcript?"

00:26:58.667 --> 00:27:00.930
You're like, "No, I don't think so."

00:27:00.930 --> 00:27:01.763
But if I call you and I was like,

00:27:01.763 --> 00:27:04.588
"Hey, by the way, this is
arriving like in two weeks."

00:27:04.588 --> 00:27:05.421
- Right.

00:27:05.421 --> 00:27:06.280
- (indistinct) to it.

00:27:06.280 --> 00:27:08.510
Or more importantly, would
you like to give us feedback

00:27:08.510 --> 00:27:09.640
when you're done?

00:27:09.640 --> 00:27:13.280
There, I think we've got a
really good uptake there.

00:27:13.280 --> 00:27:16.600
I think it also helps too that
when we started this work,

00:27:16.600 --> 00:27:18.451
I think there were a lot
of high school, excuse me,

00:27:18.451 --> 00:27:22.470
college readers who, the
concept of competency-based

00:27:22.470 --> 00:27:25.490
or master-based education
seemed a little bit abstract.

00:27:25.490 --> 00:27:27.570
They're like, "I don't know
what you're talking about."

00:27:27.570 --> 00:27:29.820
And frankly, they were
working in institutions

00:27:29.820 --> 00:27:32.960
that have decades of
institutional research data

00:27:32.960 --> 00:27:37.960
and systems that use GPA
and things like SAT and ACT,

00:27:38.100 --> 00:27:39.050
to pretty good effect.

00:27:39.050 --> 00:27:41.820
At least to do what they want them to do.

00:27:41.820 --> 00:27:43.580
Nobody was asking us for change.

00:27:43.580 --> 00:27:44.647
Like nobody came to us and said,

00:27:44.647 --> 00:27:47.510
"Hey, MTC, Can you please give
us a completely different way

00:27:47.510 --> 00:27:48.547
of doing our work?"

00:27:49.910 --> 00:27:52.900
However, what's happened in the
past couple of years, right?

00:27:52.900 --> 00:27:54.743
Well, we've had a global pandemic.

00:27:55.640 --> 00:27:58.920
Many institutions have
gotten completely moved away

00:27:58.920 --> 00:28:00.220
from standardized testing.

00:28:01.080 --> 00:28:02.350
Because of the pandemic,

00:28:02.350 --> 00:28:04.340
we've had a lot of kids
who applied to college,

00:28:04.340 --> 00:28:06.760
two years ago, with pass fail transcripts

00:28:06.760 --> 00:28:09.440
from otherwise traditional high schools.

00:28:09.440 --> 00:28:13.460
We've got a lot of kids
who may not have had access

00:28:13.460 --> 00:28:16.800
to the remote school,
hybrid school offerings

00:28:16.800 --> 00:28:19.450
that their local district
schools were offering them.

00:28:19.450 --> 00:28:21.100
And so you've got a lot of folks,

00:28:21.100 --> 00:28:24.620
who previously were pretty
content with like the status quo,

00:28:24.620 --> 00:28:26.940
suddenly now are kind
of snapped to attention.

00:28:26.940 --> 00:28:28.740
Standardized testing is changing.

00:28:28.740 --> 00:28:31.710
The concept of credit-based
on C time is changing.

00:28:31.710 --> 00:28:35.350
If as many higher ed leaders
do believe that equity

00:28:35.350 --> 00:28:37.570
is really critical to college admissions,

00:28:37.570 --> 00:28:40.220
understanding that access to education

00:28:40.220 --> 00:28:42.810
has been really inequitable
during a pandemic.

00:28:42.810 --> 00:28:45.040
All of these things we think

00:28:45.040 --> 00:28:48.108
line up in favor of us as a sector.

00:28:48.108 --> 00:28:50.470
I mean that like the K12 sector,

00:28:50.470 --> 00:28:52.110
moving towards more mastery-based,

00:28:52.110 --> 00:28:53.460
competency-based approaches.

00:28:53.460 --> 00:28:56.050
In a way that obviously the past two years

00:28:56.050 --> 00:28:58.990
have been horrible, at a national level.

00:28:58.990 --> 00:29:01.210
For a pandemic, I wouldn't
wish that on anybody,

00:29:01.210 --> 00:29:05.090
but it definitely has changed
and accelerated the way

00:29:05.090 --> 00:29:06.950
really key stakeholders
are now thinking about

00:29:06.950 --> 00:29:09.150
a lot of the stuff we're talking about.

00:29:09.150 --> 00:29:09.983
- Yeah.

00:29:09.983 --> 00:29:12.980
So let's go to a couple
questions from Facebook.

00:29:12.980 --> 00:29:15.810
Rebecca says, "Where can
you find high schools

00:29:15.810 --> 00:29:17.491
that are using a mastery curriculum?

00:29:17.491 --> 00:29:19.709
Does mastery.org have a list of those?

00:29:19.709 --> 00:29:21.750
Where can folks look?"

00:29:21.750 --> 00:29:22.583
- We definitely have a list.

00:29:22.583 --> 00:29:24.750
Every school that's part
of the MTC membership

00:29:24.750 --> 00:29:26.670
is listed publicly on our website.

00:29:26.670 --> 00:29:28.720
And I really want to be clear about this.

00:29:30.250 --> 00:29:32.480
There are many amazing
mastery-based schools

00:29:32.480 --> 00:29:33.510
that aren't part of our network,

00:29:33.510 --> 00:29:35.380
and those schools are awesome too.

00:29:35.380 --> 00:29:36.590
So I'll give a shout out

00:29:36.590 --> 00:29:38.670
to the Big Picture Learning Network,

00:29:38.670 --> 00:29:40.780
which is a network of amazing schools

00:29:40.780 --> 00:29:44.010
that are really innovating
and really pushing really hard

00:29:44.010 --> 00:29:45.650
on thinking about what
schooling looks like,

00:29:45.650 --> 00:29:47.650
and how we serve kids.

00:29:47.650 --> 00:29:50.170
There's an organization
called the Aurora Institute

00:29:50.170 --> 00:29:53.030
that publishes a map and updates.

00:29:53.030 --> 00:29:54.330
I think updates it quarterly,

00:29:54.330 --> 00:29:56.300
but definitely several times a year.

00:29:56.300 --> 00:29:57.400
And they show you

00:29:57.400 --> 00:29:59.451
what is the state of
competency-based education,

00:29:59.451 --> 00:30:01.596
each of the states.

00:30:01.596 --> 00:30:03.630
And it shows you that
there are some states

00:30:03.630 --> 00:30:05.130
that are like they're going all in.

00:30:05.130 --> 00:30:06.327
They're basically saying,

00:30:06.327 --> 00:30:09.290
"We're gonna go personalized,
competency-based

00:30:09.290 --> 00:30:11.900
across the entire state."

00:30:11.900 --> 00:30:13.680
And it's really interesting

00:30:13.680 --> 00:30:17.000
because in a world in which so much

00:30:17.000 --> 00:30:20.450
of our educational practices and discourse

00:30:20.450 --> 00:30:22.530
have gotten really politicized,

00:30:22.530 --> 00:30:26.090
there is no correlation to
the politics of a state,

00:30:26.090 --> 00:30:28.470
and its adoption of
competency-based education

00:30:28.470 --> 00:30:30.711
or mastery-based education.

00:30:30.711 --> 00:30:34.840
It makes for strange bedfellows
in a really good way.

00:30:34.840 --> 00:30:36.127
There are some folks who are saying,

00:30:36.127 --> 00:30:38.170
"Hey, this is a key driver for equity.

00:30:38.170 --> 00:30:40.240
It's about serving communities of kids,

00:30:40.240 --> 00:30:42.930
who have been traditionally
under served by our systems.

00:30:42.930 --> 00:30:44.760
We need to do better by those kids."

00:30:44.760 --> 00:30:46.897
And there's also a model that says,

00:30:46.897 --> 00:30:48.760
"We really are focused on business.

00:30:48.760 --> 00:30:50.730
We're focused on the 21st century

00:30:50.730 --> 00:30:53.320
and being a place where kids can graduate

00:30:53.320 --> 00:30:57.100
and get amazing technology
based manufacturing jobs.

00:30:57.100 --> 00:30:59.090
And we want to give them
the skills for that."

00:30:59.090 --> 00:31:00.480
And what's really interesting is

00:31:00.480 --> 00:31:01.730
then you wind up in the same place.

00:31:01.730 --> 00:31:04.730
They wind up talking together
about mastery-based learning,

00:31:04.730 --> 00:31:07.080
which, I think, is pretty great.

00:31:07.080 --> 00:31:08.050
- Yeah. That's great.

00:31:08.050 --> 00:31:10.169
And leads actually to Brian
from Facebook's question

00:31:10.169 --> 00:31:12.640
of what are the challenges involved

00:31:12.640 --> 00:31:14.340
in partnering with public schools?

00:31:15.330 --> 00:31:17.640
- Well, it depends on the public schools.

00:31:17.640 --> 00:31:20.370
So the challenge and the greatest strength

00:31:20.370 --> 00:31:22.634
and liability of our
national education system

00:31:22.634 --> 00:31:26.970
is that we have a massively
decentralized system, right?

00:31:26.970 --> 00:31:27.900
Sometimes that's awesome.

00:31:27.900 --> 00:31:32.170
It gives districts, even
individual schools and districts,

00:31:32.170 --> 00:31:34.650
a lot of autonomy to experiment.

00:31:34.650 --> 00:31:38.477
I think, we exist largely as a consortium

00:31:38.477 --> 00:31:41.100
because there's these
lighthouse districts, right?

00:31:41.100 --> 00:31:43.760
Lead by a really
inspirational leader says,

00:31:43.760 --> 00:31:45.250
we're gonna do things different.

00:31:45.250 --> 00:31:47.350
We're not gonna wait for
permission from our state,

00:31:47.350 --> 00:31:48.360
and they need a network.

00:31:48.360 --> 00:31:51.340
And so they come and
join us, which is great.

00:31:51.340 --> 00:31:54.038
But then there's also
states where the policies

00:31:54.038 --> 00:31:56.000
are just not favorable.

00:31:56.000 --> 00:31:58.790
If you are trying to
transform your district

00:31:58.790 --> 00:32:02.430
and you want to embrace
credit based on proficiency,

00:32:02.430 --> 00:32:05.090
rather than on seat
time, it's not gonna help

00:32:05.090 --> 00:32:08.600
if the law says, kids have to take X hours

00:32:08.600 --> 00:32:10.220
of social studies in order to graduate.

00:32:10.220 --> 00:32:13.070
You need someone to give
you permission to do that.

00:32:13.070 --> 00:32:15.462
And so those are probably
the biggest challenges.

00:32:15.462 --> 00:32:18.110
It's not challenges
specific to public schools,

00:32:18.110 --> 00:32:19.510
as public schools.

00:32:19.510 --> 00:32:21.987
It's differing rates of adoption

00:32:21.987 --> 00:32:24.730
of favorable policy frameworks

00:32:24.730 --> 00:32:26.620
in different states and localities.

00:32:26.620 --> 00:32:28.630
And again, sometimes
that works in your favor.

00:32:28.630 --> 00:32:31.590
Sometimes there'll be
states, Utah, South Carolina.

00:32:31.590 --> 00:32:33.650
There's a lot of interest
in Kentucky right now,

00:32:33.650 --> 00:32:37.080
where they'll create really
favorable policy frameworks

00:32:37.080 --> 00:32:39.980
and provide funding, and then,
it can be off to the races.

00:32:41.330 --> 00:32:42.720
- Fantastic.

00:32:42.720 --> 00:32:47.720
We have already 30 minutes
in, and at time quickly.

00:32:48.130 --> 00:32:50.090
It sounds like the work you're doing,

00:32:50.090 --> 00:32:55.090
both really unblocks one of
the big concerns or objections

00:32:55.860 --> 00:32:58.100
that folks have, which
is, oh, if we do this,

00:32:58.100 --> 00:33:00.970
how are we going to be able
to get students into college

00:33:00.970 --> 00:33:04.513
and where those is, which
sounds like one of those pieces.

00:33:04.513 --> 00:33:07.880
I'm gonna end on one more question

00:33:07.880 --> 00:33:11.350
that came from YouTube,
Nasser Grunono says,

00:33:11.350 --> 00:33:13.573
what are all those medals for?

00:33:14.630 --> 00:33:17.673
- So they are thank
you, Nasser, out there.

00:33:19.680 --> 00:33:21.410
None of them is a victory medal.

00:33:21.410 --> 00:33:23.050
And I think that's really important.

00:33:23.050 --> 00:33:25.100
And hopefully, on brand
for what I'm talking about.

00:33:25.100 --> 00:33:29.510
We're not against, we're an
antis sorting organization.

00:33:29.510 --> 00:33:31.460
They're finishers medals
for different races

00:33:31.460 --> 00:33:33.170
of different lengths and different types.

00:33:33.170 --> 00:33:34.220
And I keep 'em up there

00:33:34.220 --> 00:33:36.620
because I think they're
pretty good metaphor

00:33:36.620 --> 00:33:40.180
or analogy for grading based on mastery.

00:33:40.180 --> 00:33:42.670
If you finish, even if it
takes you a little bit longer,

00:33:42.670 --> 00:33:43.890
you still get that.

00:33:43.890 --> 00:33:45.790
And if you get a wide
enough array of them,

00:33:45.790 --> 00:33:47.330
for different lengths
and different events,

00:33:47.330 --> 00:33:49.820
it starts to tell a
story of what you can do.

00:33:49.820 --> 00:33:53.207
And so I keep 'em up there
as a kind of a reminder

00:33:53.207 --> 00:33:54.320
of what we're trying to do.

00:33:54.320 --> 00:33:56.610
Give kids evidence that
even if it takes 'em

00:33:56.610 --> 00:33:58.100
a little bit longer to get to proficiency

00:33:58.100 --> 00:34:00.310
or mastery in certain areas,

00:34:00.310 --> 00:34:02.260
as a long as they got there, it's good.

00:34:03.920 --> 00:34:04.850
- Fantastic.

00:34:04.850 --> 00:34:06.624
Thanks so much for the work you're doing,

00:34:06.624 --> 00:34:08.790
and look forward to seeing more

00:34:08.790 --> 00:34:11.050
from the Mastery Transcript Consortium.

00:34:11.050 --> 00:34:12.760
Thanks for being with us today.

00:34:12.760 --> 00:34:13.593
- Thank you so much.

00:34:13.593 --> 00:34:14.426
Spend delight.

00:34:15.350 --> 00:34:16.183
- Excellent.

00:34:16.183 --> 00:34:17.016
And to all of you,

00:34:17.016 --> 00:34:19.226
we actually have another Ed Talk tomorrow

